From 38061c219e0fd4adbb7b85ac518527785ff6864c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Claude (draft)" <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 16:17:06 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] mm/vmscan: split large anon folios in direct reclaim to
 zram

Swapping out a large anon folio to zram (or any SWP_SYNCHRONOUS_IO
device, or zswap) compresses every page of it in the reclaiming task:
swap_writeout() -> submit_bio_wait() runs the compressor right there.
For a 2 MiB THP with zstd that is 3-4 ms. MGLRU isolates a single THP as
a whole batch (scan_folios() stops at MIN_LRU_BATCH pages), so every
direct reclaim pass that lands on THP-backed anon memory compresses a
full 2 MiB, while an order-0 allocation needs SWAP_CLUSTER_MAX pages.

With THP enabled=always, almost all anon memory is THP-backed. Measured
on a 32 GiB desktop with 16 GiB zram (zstd), swappiness 150, while a
program allocated several GiB within seconds: 1243 direct reclaim
passes over 1 ms in 5 s, 972 of them reclaiming 512+ pages at 3.7 ms on
average, in whatever task happened to allocate: pipewire and
pipewire-pulse, the browser, the compositor of a video call client,
systemd-journald, kworkers. The memory those 3.7 ms free goes to
whoever allocates next.

In direct reclaim, split such a folio instead, reclaim about as many
pages as the caller still needs from its start (an aligned, physically
contiguous range, which also serves small higher-order requests) plus
any pages the split left unmapped, and return the other pages to the
LRU for kswapd. A pass then compresses ~32 pages (a fraction of a ms)
plus the split (unmap and remap of the folio under the anon_vma lock,
tens of us). If the caller already reclaimed enough in this pass, the
folio is kept. If the split fails, the folio is swapped out whole as
before, so this never makes reclaim fail.

Only for synchronous swap-out (swap_out_is_synchronous(): zswap, or the
device the per-CPU swap cluster cache or the highest priority available
device points to), and not for kswapd, proactive reclaim or
MADV_PAGEOUT/DAMON (ignore_references), which still swap out large
folios whole. Requests that need half the folio or more (high-order
compaction gaps, large memcg reclaim targets) are not split either.

Costs: the split huge page is gone for the rest of its pages too, they
are swapped out (and back in) as small pages later, and khugepaged only
collapses the range again once its swapped-out pages are back. MGLRU
puts the returned pages into a young generation, like any anon folio
that reclaim rejected, so they stay in memory a while longer. Under
sustained pressure, direct reclaim splits one huge page per ~32 pages
it needs instead of compressing one per 512.

Controlled by vm.thp_direct_reclaim_split (default 0), counted in
/proc/vmstat as thp_swpout_direct_split.

DRAFT for linux-lunar; not submitted upstream. Not compiled or run yet.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
